>> title: Raita
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: indian, yogurt, cucumber, cooling, condiment
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: raitha, cucumber raita, kheera raita, dahi raita, boondi raita
>> pairs-with: biryani, vindaloo, mango-chutney
>> servings: 6
>> time: 50 min
>> step.1: salt and stand 15 min, then wring it out
>> step.2: toast 1 min, then crush
>> step.3: whisk 1 min, until it pours
>> step.4: fold in and chill 30 min

Rest @cucumber{1%large}(coarsely grated, skin on) under @kosher salt{1/2%tsp} ~stand{15%min}, then wring it out in a cloth over the sink, hard. A raita made with unwrung cucumber is a bowl of thin white water within the hour.

Toast @cumin seeds{2%tsp} in a dry #skillet{} ~toast{1%min}, until they darken, then crush them coarsely in a #mortar{}.

Whisk @&(~1)roasted cumin{} into @plain whole-milk yogurt{2%cup}(480 g), @kosher salt{3/4%tsp} and @granulated sugar{1/2%tsp} ~whisk{1%min}, until it is smooth enough to pour off a spoon.

Fold @&(~3)squeezed cucumber{} into @&(~1)yogurt{} with @mint{2%Tbs}(chopped) and @chaat masala{1/2%tsp}, and ~chill{30%min}.

This is what you reach for when a vindaloo has gone wrong, and it works because capsaicin dissolves in fat and casein carries it off the tongue — water and beer do neither, which is why the second pint does not help. Whole-milk yogurt for that reason. It is also the standing partner to biryani, where it is less a remedy than half the meal. Boondi raita swaps the cucumber for little fried chickpea-flour pearls dropped in at the last minute.
